---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import { identityConfig } from "../lib/env.mjs";
import agentPrompt from "../data/agent-prompt.json";

// Where a submission is built and sent. Two ways to fill it in, and neither is
// the real one.
//
// Premise, restated because a premise stated in one file gets violated in every
// other one: **plurality is the product; the registry never designates,
// consumers pin, visibly.** Nothing on this page ranks, scores, approves or
// filters a submission, there is no queue and there is nothing to approve: the
// namespace is the handle the provider reported, so there is no question for a
// reviewer to answer.
//
// **This page is a split out of `/signed-in/`, taken deliberately.** That page
// was the return leg and the submission form at once, and the cost showed up in
// the bar: **Add artifact** pointed at it, so pressing it while the return leg
// was still running reloaded the page and threw away the exchange in progress.
// Splitting them is the fix rather than a tidy-up. `/signed-in/` receives the
// redirect and reads what the provider said; this page holds a record somebody
// is still typing. The split is only possible because the session persists: a
// page that is not the return leg can know who is signed in, off one key in the
// reader's own browser, through `astro/src/lib/held.mjs`.
//
// **The upload offer's second authorization comes back through `/signed-in/`.**
// The redirect URL registered with the identity service is that page and only
// that page, so the popup lands there, and that page relays the code to
// whichever window opened it and closes. No console change, and nothing here
// depends on one. The exchange happens in this window, against the verifier
// this window wrote, exactly as it did when both halves lived on one page.
//
// **Two routes in, and one of them is not checked here.** Somebody with a
// coding agent copies a prompt, runs it where the extraction happened, and
// pastes what comes back. That paste crosses as text and **nothing in this
// browser reads it**: `artifacts/agent_handoff.py` holds the parser, in Python,
// and it runs when the author pulls the row in. So a mistake in a paste
// surfaces then rather than as somebody types, which is a real difference from
// the field route and is said on the page in those words. What it buys is that
// there is one parser rather than two that drift, which is the failure this
// repository has hit most often.
//
// **The prompt is generated, never typed here.** `artifacts/intake.py prompt`
// writes `astro/src/data/agent-prompt.json` during `make site`, out of
// `agent_handoff.prompt(suggestions(conn))`, so the text a reader copies is the
// text the parser was written against. `tests/test_submit_page.py` holds the
// built page to that function, so the two cannot come apart.
//
// **This page writes a row, and the row is not the corpus.** A completed
// submission is sent to `pending_submission` over the submitter's own session.
// It is not a queue and there is nothing to approve. The published corpus is
// still a file in git, a submission appears on the site when the author pulls
// it in and publishes a rebuild, and that is what makes the falsifier mean
// anything.
//
// **The identity on that row is stamped by Postgres and never read off the
// form.** The insert policy refuses a row whose `account`, `subject` or
// `handle` disagrees with the verified session, so a submission cannot claim to
// be from somebody it is not. The record inside still carries the browser's own
// copy of both, unedited, and where the two disagree the author's pull says so.
//
// **The copy is drafted, not dictated.** Structure and accuracy are mine; every
// sentence a reader sees is the author's to rewrite.
//
// ---
//
// **2026-09-20, the layout pass. Nothing below changed what the page collects
// or what it says.** The page went live as one column carrying a prompt to
// copy, a paste box, twenty-two fields, an absences box and an upload offer,
// all at the same level, so somebody arriving to publish one direction had to
// work out which of those applied to them before they could start.
//
// **The two ways in are a choice the page makes, rather than one it refuses
// after the fact.** They are mutually exclusive: `chosenRoute` refuses a
// record that is both, and it refused it at the end, after somebody had filled
// in both. They are now two `<details>` sharing a `name`, which is the
// platform's own exclusive accordion: opening one closes the other, no script
// runs, and an engine that does not know the attribute renders two ordinary
// disclosures and loses nothing. Neither is open on arrival, because opening
// one would be this page naming the ordinary way to do it, and the page's own
// sentence says neither is the better one.
//
// **The banner about a paste not being checked did not move into a
// disclosure.** It is the thing a reader needs in order to choose, so it sits
// above the pair with the sentence about doing both, where it is visible
// whichever way is open. Same for the holding-table banner, which is the other
// statement on this page that has to survive skimming.
//
// **The upload offer is inside the artifact fieldset now.** It fills in the
// repository, the commit and the path, and it sat between the fields and the
// send button, at the same level as both, where it read as a step everybody
// takes rather than an offer for somebody whose repository is private. Its own
// copy already said so: "part of filling the fields in rather than something
// to do beside a paste".
//
// **A field that may be absent says so on its label**, and the flag comes off
// what `submissionFrom` does with it: the nine it passes through `optional()`
// are the nine marked. A reader can now tell those from the ones a record
// cannot be built without, which rendered identically. The word sits inside
// the `<label>` rather than beside it, so a screen reader reads it with the
// field rather than as a stray word in the row.
//
// **The explanations moved beside the fields rather than under them.** A note
// under every one of twenty-two fields is a wall with nothing to scan; in a
// second column the left edge is label, box, label, box, and the prose is
// still there for whoever stops.
//
// ---
//
// **2026-09-20, reading back what this account has sent.** The receipt was a
// row id and a timestamp, and then nothing until the author published a
// rebuild. `What you have sent` is the records themselves, fetched in the
// browser over the reader's own session, the one just sent included.
//
// **Nothing was widened to do it.** The select policy on
// `schema/supabase/001_pending_submission.sql` has always been
// `account = auth.uid()`: a signed-in person reads their own rows and nobody
// else's, and an anonymous caller gets an empty list. There is no view, no
// public read and no schema change.
//
// **The whole risk here is that a played-back record reads as a corpus page**,
// and one step further, that a list of them reads as a queue somebody is
// working through. So: the region says in its first sentence, and every record
// says again, that it is not in the corpus, that nothing has checked it and
// that nobody else can read it. Every value is quoted and sits in a
// `data-authored` region, which is the mark the falsifier reads on a published
// page to tell an author's number from one this registry derived, and a number
// in a record is exactly that. `author/model/label@version` is never printed,
// because it resolves to a frozen submission and this resolves to nothing.
// `taken_at` renders as read in, in the words `artifacts/intake.py` uses, and
// never as accepted. The one sequence is the clock, named on screen and
// reversible on a press, which is the cheapest demonstration that it carries
// nothing: `inArrivalOrder` reads no field of any record.
//
// **The rows are wiped on Sign out rather than hidden.** The next person to
// sign in on this browser is a different person, and a hidden element is still
// an element.

const { url: supabaseUrl, key: supabaseKey, configured } = identityConfig();

// Addressed rather than enumerated. A custom OIDC provider in Supabase is named
// `custom:<name>`, and this is the one that is configured. Nothing anywhere
// compares it against a set of names.
const PROVIDER = "custom:huggingface";

// The fields the schema takes, each with why it is there. Free text, every one
// of them: nothing on this page checks a value against a set, and a value
// nobody has used before is the ordinary way a corpus grows.
//
// The fifth column is whether the record can be built without this one, and it
// is not a judgment about the field. It is `submissionFrom` read off: the nine
// it puts through `optional()` or `asNumber()` accept nothing and record null,
// and the rest throw naming themselves. Written here rather than derived from
// the sentence beside it, because three of the nine do not say the word and
// `license_status` says the opposite of short.
const MAY_BE_ABSENT = true;
const CONTRACT = [
  {
    group: "The submission",
    fields: [
      ["label", "label", "text",
        "The thing other people will disagree with you about. A bare label is a view across everyone claiming it, owned by nobody."],
      ["version", "version", "text",
        "author/model/label@version resolves to one frozen submission forever, so there has to be a version to freeze."],
      ["created_at", "created at", "text",
        "When the work happened, which is not when it was typed in here."],
      ["definition", "your definition", "textarea",
        "Load bearing twice: it feeds contrast-pair generation and it is the thing another author disagrees with."],
    ],
  },
  {
    group: "The intervention",
    fields: [
      ["intervention_id", "intervention id", "text",
        "The primary key, and the thing an eval report or an attack points at."],
      ["kind", "kind", "text",
        "An open string. Whatever you call this. Nothing checks it against a list."],
      ["model_id", "model id", "text",
        "A direction has no meaning apart from the model it was read out of, which is why the model is part of the identity."],
      ["model_revision", "model revision", "text",
        "Optional. Absent renders as absent rather than as an error.", MAY_BE_ABSENT],
      ["layer", "layer", "text",
        "A whole number. A band across several layers cannot be stated in this column at all, which is a recorded gap."],
      ["layer_convention", "layer convention", "text",
        "Getting this wrong is silent. block-0indexed is a common value and not the only one."],
      ["hook_point", "hook point", "text",
        "An open string. Where in the block this acts."],
      ["chat_template_hash", "chat template hash", "text",
        "Optional.", MAY_BE_ABSENT],
      ["activation_norm", "activation norm", "text",
        "Optional. A number, or nothing.", MAY_BE_ABSENT],
      ["coeff_low", "coefficient, low", "text", "Optional.", MAY_BE_ABSENT],
      ["coeff_high", "coefficient, high", "text", "Optional.", MAY_BE_ABSENT],
      ["steering_position", "steering position", "text", "Optional.", MAY_BE_ABSENT],
      ["license_status", "license status", "textarea",
        "What the source model's license says about an artifact derived from it, with the URL and the date you read it. Yours to assert, not ours to decide.",
        MAY_BE_ABSENT],
    ],
  },
  {
    group: "The artifact, which is a link",
    // The upload offer renders inside this fieldset. It fills in the three
    // fields above it and nothing else, which is what makes it part of this
    // group rather than a step of its own between the form and the send.
    offer: true,
    fields: [
      ["repo", "repository", "text",
        "owner/name. The bytes stay yours and this records where they are."],
      ["commit", "commit", "text",
        "Forty hex characters. A branch or a tag is movable by whoever owns the repository, so neither can be pinned."],
      ["path", "path in the repository", "text",
        "The same string the client will fetch by."],
      ["host", "host", "text",
        "Optional. Empty means the Hub, which is where a row recording no host has always resolved.", MAY_BE_ABSENT],
      ["url_template", "url template", "text",
        "Optional, and how the four fields above become a URL on a host this project has never met.", MAY_BE_ABSENT],
    ],
  },
];
---

<Base title="Submit a direction" trail={[{ text: "controlbun", href: "/" }]} synthetic="none">
  <h1 slot="heading">Submit a direction</h1>

  <div
    id="handshake"
    data-supabase-url={supabaseUrl}
    data-supabase-key={supabaseKey}
    data-provider={PROVIDER}
  >
  </div>

  {!configured && (
    <p class="banner">
      <strong>This build was made without an identity endpoint.</strong> There
      is nothing to press on this page, because a button that cannot work is
      worse than no button. The endpoint is read at build time from a file this
      repository does not carry.
    </p>
  )}

  <p class="lede">
    A submission is one author's complete take on one label against one model,
    frozen at <code>author/model/label@version</code> forever. Yours does not
    replace anybody's and nobody's replaces yours. There are two ways to fill
    this in and neither is the better one: type it, or hand a prompt to the
    coding agent that already has your extraction scripts open.
  </p>

  {/* Everything below this line is assembled and sent by a script, so with
      scripting off the page is a heading and a lede. That was the whole of it
      until now: no state, no words, nothing to do. This says which, and does
      not offer a way round it, because there is not one. Mail is not a
      submission route and the send path says so in those words. */}
  <noscript>
    <p class="state">
      <strong>This page builds a submission in the browser, so it needs
      JavaScript.</strong>{" "}
      The record is assembled here and sent from here, and there is no page on
      this site that receives one instead. Everything else here reads with
      scripting off, including{" "}
      <a href="/sign-in/">what signing in is for</a>.
    </p>
    <p class="state">
      <strong>Reading back what you have already sent needs it too.</strong>{" "}
      Those records are fetched from the holding table in the browser, with your
      own session, because the published pages are files a build wrote and none
      of them holds anybody's unpublished record. With scripting off there is
      nothing here to show you and no other page on this site that has it.
    </p>
  </noscript>

  {/* The five states this page can be in before there is anything to fill in.
      Reading a session and remembering one are progress, a refused renewal and
      a failed exchange are failures, and having no session at all is neither.
      Four of the five rendered in the same amber, next to a fifth amber block
      about the holding table, so the one statement on this page that has to
      survive skimming was competing with two that are not warnings at all.

      Being signed out is the ordinary state of a first visit. Same rule this
      project applies to an absent eval: a state gets a rendering of its own
      rather than an error's. Nothing said here changed. */}
  <p id="working" hidden class="state" role="status">Reading the session this browser kept.</p>

  <p id="failed" hidden class="banner" role="alert"></p>

  {/* Nobody is signed in here. Its own state with its own words, and a way on
      rather than a dead end. */}
  <p id="nosession" hidden class="state">
    <strong>There is no session in this browser.</strong> A submission is sent
    as somebody, because the database stamps the namespace from the session
    rather than taking it from a field. <a href="/signed-in/">Sign in with
    Hugging Face</a>, which comes back and sends you here, or read{" "}
    <a href="/sign-in/">what signing in is for</a> first. Reading this site
    needs none of it.
  </p>

  <p id="remembered" hidden class="state" role="status"></p>

  <p id="stale" hidden class="banner" role="alert"></p>

  <section id="submit-section" class="submitting" hidden>
    <p class="banner">
      <strong>A submission does not appear on this site until the author
      rebuilds and publishes it.</strong> The corpus is a file in git and the
      site is built from it on one machine, which is what lets the falsifier
      check the thing readers actually read. Pressing send puts what you wrote
      in a holding table, where it waits to be read. That is not a queue and
      there is nothing to approve: your namespace is the handle Hugging Face
      reported, so there is no question for anybody to answer about it.
    </p>

    <p>
      Your namespace is <strong id="namespace-shown"></strong>, which is the
      handle Hugging Face reported when you signed in. There is no field for it
      and no default to type over. What that makes impossible: publishing under
      a pseudonym, under a lab name you hold no account for, or into one
      namespace shared by several people who each sign in as themselves.
    </p>

    <p class="note">
      A submission is a pointer to bytes a stranger can fetch. If your
      repository is private, the section further down will publish the one file
      to your own account, with your permission, and then fill in the pin.
    </p>

    {/* The banner that has to survive being skimmed, and the sentence about
        doing both, sit above the choice rather than inside the half of it they
        describe. A reader picking between the two needs the asymmetry between
        them before they pick, and the banner is what states it. The constraint
        is the same one `CLAUDE.md` puts on an absent eval: the statement is the
        point, so it does not go behind a click. */}
    <p class="banner">
      {/* The space is written rather than left to the newline: Astro drops the
          leading whitespace of a text node that starts a line, and this banner
          rendered as "pulls it in.Nothing in this browser" on the live site. */}
      <strong>A paste is not checked until the author pulls it in.</strong>{" "}
      Nothing in this browser reads what you paste. The parser is one piece of
      Python and it runs on the author's machine when the row is read in, so a
      paste that is missing a field or carries a placeholder comes back to you
      then, by mail, rather than the moment you paste it. The fields below are
      the other way round: a mistake in one of those surfaces as you type. The
      reason there is only one parser is that two of them drift, and a paste
      that parsed in a browser and refused on the author's machine is worse
      than waiting.
    </p>

    <p class="note">
      Pasting and filling the fields in at once is refused, and neither is
      preferred. Those are two accounts of one submission and nothing here can
      tell which was meant, which is the same reason a field cannot carry both
      a value and a reason for having none.
    </p>

    {/* Two `<details>` sharing a `name`, which is HTML's exclusive accordion:
        opening one closes the other, with no script and no state of our own.
        Shipped in Chrome and Edge 120, Safari 17.2, Firefox 130 (MDN
        browser-compat-data, html/elements/details.json, read 2026-09-20). An
        engine older than those ignores the attribute and renders two ordinary
        disclosures, which is what this page did on every engine yesterday, so
        the floor is the previous behavior rather than a broken one.

        Neither carries `open`. A default here would be the page naming the
        ordinary way to do this, on a page whose own lede says neither is the
        better one.

        Closed is not unreachable: find-in-page opens a closed `<details>` in
        Chrome and Edge 97+, Firefox 139+ and Safari 26.2 (same source, the
        `search_match_opens` subfeature), and the text is in the document
        either way for anything reading the page rather than rendering it. */}
    <div class="routes">
      <details class="route" id="handoff" name="route" data-route>
        <summary><h2>Hand it to your coding agent</h2></summary>
        <div class="route-body">
          <p>
            An agent sitting in the checkout where the extraction happened can
            read most of this off your own scripts, which is where the answers
            are. Copy the prompt, run it there, and paste back what it
            produces. The prompt tells it what each field is for and tells it,
            in the strongest terms the text can manage, never to write a value
            it did not read.
          </p>

          <p class="act">
            <button type="button" id="copy-prompt" class="go">Copy the prompt</button>{" "}
            <span class="note" id="copy-said" role="status"></span>
          </p>

          <details id="prompt-shown">
            <summary>Read the prompt first</summary>
            <p class="note">
              This is the whole of what gets copied. It is generated from the
              same field list the parser reads, so what it asks for and what the
              parser takes cannot come apart.
            </p>
            <pre id="agent-prompt-text" data-pagefind-ignore>{agentPrompt.prompt}</pre>
          </details>

          <div class="field stacked">
            <label for="f-pasted">what your agent produced</label>
            <textarea id="f-pasted" rows="8" spellcheck="false"
                      aria-describedby="why-pasted"></textarea>
            <p class="why" id="why-pasted">
              Paste the whole reply, including anything it wrote to you around
              the block. The parser finds the block inside surrounding prose and
              reads past the rest, so explanation costs nothing.
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </details>

      <details class="route" id="byhand" name="route" data-route>
        <summary><h2>Or fill it in yourself</h2></summary>
        <div class="route-body">
          <p class="note">
            Every field is an open string. Nothing here is checked against a
            list of permitted values, and a value nobody has used before is the
            ordinary way this corpus grows.
          </p>

          <div id="fields">
            {CONTRACT.map((group) => (
              <fieldset>
                <legend>{group.group}</legend>
                {group.fields.map(([name, title, kind, why, mayBeAbsent]) => (
                  <div class="field">
                    {/* The space is an expression because Astro emits the two
                        adjacent ones with nothing between them, and a screen
                        reader then reads "model revisionoptional". */}
                    <label for={`f-${name}`}>
                      {title}{" "}
                      {mayBeAbsent && <span class="may">optional</span>}
                    </label>
                    {kind === "textarea" ? (
                      <textarea id={`f-${name}`} name={name} rows="3"
                                aria-describedby={`why-${name}`}></textarea>
                    ) : (
                      <input id={`f-${name}`} name={name} type="text"
                             aria-describedby={`why-${name}`} />
                    )}
                    <p class="why" id={`why-${name}`}>{why}</p>
                  </div>
                ))}

                {group.offer && (
                  <details id="offer">
                    <summary>The file is not at a public URL yet</summary>
                    <p>
                      This registry can put one file in{" "}
                      <strong id="offer-namespace"></strong>, your own account,
                      in a new public repository, and then fill in the
                      repository, the commit and the path above. The bytes go to
                      your account and never to this project's, which is a
                      different column in the schema and a different question.
                    </p>
                    <p>
                      Hugging Face will ask you for one more permission at that
                      point and not before: creating repositories for this app,
                      which cannot reach any other repository you own. It asks
                      every time, including on a visit where this browser
                      already had your session, because the token that
                      permission returns is the one credential here that is
                      never stored, logged or written anywhere. It lives in one
                      variable for the length of the upload.
                    </p>
                    <p class="note">
                      Taking this offer fills three fields in, so it is part of
                      filling the fields in rather than something to do beside a
                      paste. An agent that knows where the file will be
                      published answers those three itself.
                    </p>
                    <div class="field">
                      <label for="f-upload-name">repository to create</label>
                      <input id="f-upload-name" type="text"
                             aria-describedby="why-upload-name" />
                      <p class="why" id="why-upload-name">A new one, under your
                        namespace. An existing one stops the offer rather than
                        being written into.</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="field">
                      <label for="f-upload-path">path inside it</label>
                      <input id="f-upload-path" type="text"
                             aria-describedby="why-upload-path" />
                      <p class="why" id="why-upload-path">The same string the
                        client will fetch by, so it is also the path recorded on
                        the row.</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="field">
                      <label for="f-upload-file">the file</label>
                      <input id="f-upload-file" type="file"
                             aria-describedby="why-upload-file" />
                      <p class="why" id="why-upload-file">It is read in this tab
                        and sent to Hugging Face. It does not reach this site,
                        which has nowhere to receive it.</p>
                    </div>
                    <p class="act">
                      <button type="button" id="do-upload">Ask permission and
                        publish it to my account</button>
                    </p>
                    <p id="upload-said" class="note" role="status"></p>
                  </details>
                )}
              </fieldset>
            ))}
          </div>

          <details id="absences">
            <summary>Something is absent and you want to say why</summary>
            <p class="note">
              An absence is a positive statement with a reason, never an
              omission and never an error. One line per field, as{" "}
              <code>field: your sentence</code>. The field name is any string;
              it is not checked against a list. An agent writes these itself, on
              the <code>not-found:</code> lines the prompt asks for, so this box
              belongs to the fields above.
            </p>
            <div class="field stacked">
              <label for="f-absent">absences</label>
              <textarea id="f-absent" rows="3" aria-describedby="why-absent"></textarea>
              <p class="why" id="why-absent">A field cannot carry both a value
                above and a reason for having none. That is two claims about one
                field, and the author's rebuild refuses it rather than picking
                one.</p>
            </div>
          </details>
        </div>
      </details>
    </div>

    <p class="act finish">
      <button type="button" id="build-record" class="go">Build my submission</button>
    </p>
    <p id="record-said" class="banner" hidden role="alert"></p>

    <div id="record-out" class="outcome" hidden>
      <h3>Your submission</h3>
      <p>
        Nothing has been sent yet. This is the record. Read it, then send it.
      </p>
      <pre id="record-json"></pre>
      <p class="act">
        <button type="button" id="send-record" class="go">Send it</button>{" "}
        <button type="button" id="keep-record">Download a copy</button>
      </p>
      <p class="note">
        Sending puts this record and three columns beside it in a holding table:
        the account, the subject and the handle, all three taken from your signed
        session by the database rather than from anything above. So a submission
        cannot claim to be from somebody it is not. The copy is for you; it is
        not needed for anything and nothing here depends on your keeping it.
      </p>
      <p class="note">
        Reading it later fetches the bytes at the pin, checks them against what
        this record says, writes the row and rebuilds the site. The shape, the
        dtype, the norm and the digest are read off the bytes on that machine
        rather than computed here, because one thing in this project reads bytes
        and a second implementation of it is the failure this repository has hit
        most often. A pin nobody can fetch is refused there, with the reason. A
        paste is read there too, and by the same argument.
      </p>
    </div>

    <section id="sent" class="outcome" hidden>
      <h3>Received</h3>
      <p class="banner">
        <strong>It is in the holding table and it is not on the site.</strong>{" "}
        It appears when the author reads it in, rebuilds the corpus and
        publishes, because the corpus is a file in git. Nothing was approved and
        nothing was ranked.
      </p>
      <dl class="said">
        <dt>received at</dt><dd id="sent-when"></dd>
        <dt>row</dt><dd id="sent-id"></dd>
        <dt>stamped handle</dt><dd id="sent-handle"></dd>
        <dt>stamped subject</dt><dd id="sent-sub"></dd>
      </dl>
      <p class="note">
        Those four are what the database wrote, read back off its answer rather
